    },
    {
      "label": "4",
      "number": "4",
      "statement": "A pump fills $1.8$ litres in $45$ seconds at a steady rate. Express its flow in millilitres per second and litres per minute.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    },
    {
      "label": "5",
      "number": "5",
      "statement": "A box has mass $12$ kg. What is its weight? State any assumption needed; distinguish mass from weight.",
      "page": null,
      "parts": [],
      "parts_relation": "none"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Convert distance to metres",
      "content": "The distance is $7.5\\text{ km}$. Since $1\\text{ km} = 1000\\text{ m}$,\n$$7.5\\text{ km} = 7.5 \\times 1000 = 7500\\text{ m}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Convert time to seconds",
      "content": "The time is $25\\text{ min}$. Since $1\\text{ min} = 60\\text{ s}$,\n$$25\\text{ min} = 25 \\times 60 = 1500\\text{ s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Compute speed",
      "content": "Speed is distance divided by time:\n$$v = \\frac{7500\\text{ m}}{1500\\text{ s}} = 5\\text{ m/s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "The speed is $5\\text{ m/s}$."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Part (a) Mean of the reading times",
      "content": "No course context was provided, so this uses general knowledge. The data are $2,4,4,10$ minutes. The mean is the sum divided by the number of observations: $$\\bar{x}=\\frac{2+4+4+10}{4}=\\frac{20}{4}=5.$$ Thus the mean reading time is $5$ minutes.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Part (b) Median of the reading times",
      "content": "The data are already ordered: $2,4,4,10$. With $n=4$ observations, the median is the average of the two middle values, the second and third values: $$\\text{median}=\\frac{4+4}{2}=4.$$ Thus the median reading time is $4$ minutes.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Part (c) Choosing the better typical measure",
      "content": "The median better describes a typical observation here. The value $10$ minutes is much larger than the other values and pulls the mean upward to $5$ minutes, while two of the four observations are exactly $4$ minutes and the remaining observations are $2$ and $10$ minutes. The median is less affected by this high outlier, so $4$ minutes is more representative of a typical daily reading time.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Mean: $5$ minutes. Median: $4$ minutes. The median better describes a typical observation because the $10$-minute value is an outlier that skews the mean upward."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Identify the variable being tested",
      "content": "The experiment changes only light exposure, so light is the independent variable and plant growth is the dependent variable. Soil, water, species, and age are controlled variables.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Explain why controlling variables helps",
      "content": "Holding soil, water, species, and age constant reduces confounding variables. If those factors differed between groups, a difference in growth could be caused by better soil, more water, a different species, or age rather than by light. Controlling them makes it more reasonable to compare growth between light conditions.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Assess whether one taller plant proves causation",
      "content": "No. One taller plant in the light group alone does not prove that light caused the growth. That result could come from random variation, measurement error, an uncontrolled factor, or chance. To support a causal claim, the experiment should use multiple plants, random assignment, replication, and a statistically reliable difference between groups.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Controlling soil, water, species, and age helps isolate light as the possible cause of growth differences. However, a single taller plant in the light group alone does not prove that light caused growth; replicated controlled experiments and statistical evidence are needed."
}

{
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Convert litres to millilitres and find millilitres per second",
      "content": "No course context was provided, so this step uses general unit conversion. Since $1\\text{ L}=1000\\text{ mL}$, the volume is $1.8\\text{ L}=1800\\text{ mL}$. The steady flow rate is\n$$\\frac{1800\\text{ mL}}{45\\text{ s}}=40\\text{ mL/s}.$$",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Convert seconds to minutes and find litres per minute",
      "content": "Using $60\\text{ s}=1\\text{ min}$, convert the rate from litres per second to litres per minute:\n$$\\frac{1.8\\text{ L}}{45\\text{ s}}\\times\\frac{60\\text{ s}}{1\\text{ min}}=2.4\\text{ L/min}.$$",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "The flow is $40\\text{ mL/s}$ and $2.4\\text{ L/min}$."
}

{
  "verdict": "agrees",
